This is fine and works, since you have to repeat this is in other functions as well, I'd do:

```go
func (e *Executor) getJob(id uuid.UUID) (*proc, error) {
	e.mu.RLock()
	defer e.mu.RUnlock()

	p, ok := e.processes[id]
	if !ok {
		return nil, fmt.Errorf("not found")
	}

	return p, nil
}
```

As it stands, if a user is creating multiple processes concurrently, all of the processes will get started but they will get synchronized before the id gets returned to the user. The global user lock needs to happen so as if a user has never created a process before tries to create multiple concurrent processes a race doesnt happen where one of the processes could get lost.

I've changed it now so the process set for a user can also be separately locked and then the write becomes this:
```go
func (h *handlers) updateOrCreateUserProcesses(user string, id uuid.UUID) {
	h.procLock.Lock() // stops the concurrent start race
	userProcs, ok := h.userProcs[user]
	if !ok {
		userProcs = &procs{p: map[uuid.UUID]struct{}{}}
		h.userProcs[user] = userProcs
	}
	h.procLock.Unlock()

	userProcs.Lock()
	defer userProcs.Unlock()
	userProcs.p[id] = struct{}{}
}
```
I'm not sure if this is better or worse really, or if there is a better way. If we consider the actual list of users to be constant (as they are in this case, but would not be in production) all of them could be initialized at startup and then the above could become something like this:

```go
func (h *handlers) updateOrCreateUserProcesses(user string, id uuid.UUID) {
	h.procLock.RLock() // stops the concurrent start race
	userProcs := h.userProcs[user]
	h.procLock.RUnlock()
	userProcs.Lock()
	defer userProcs.Unlock()
	userProcs.p[id] = struct{}{}
}
```
